# example_data/

This directory is where the notebook reads data from. On first run, the
notebook's `load_data("example_data/")` call will download a subset of MNIST
into this directory and save it as `mnist_subset.pt`. Subsequent runs reload
that file directly.

## Use your own data

Drop a file (or pair of files) into this directory matching one of three
conventions. `load_data` checks formats in this priority order: `.pt` → `.json`
→ `.csv` → fall back to MNIST.

### Option 1 — `.pt` (fastest)

A single `.pt` file (any name) containing a dict:

```python
torch.save({
    "x_train": ...,  # (N_train, input_dim) float tensor
    "y_train": ...,  # (N_train,) long tensor of labels in [0, n_classes)
    "x_test":  ...,  # (N_test, input_dim) float tensor
    "y_test":  ...,  # (N_test,) long tensor
}, "example_data/my_dataset.pt")
```

If you want to override the bundled `mnist_subset.pt`, either delete it or name
your file so it sorts first alphabetically.

### Option 2 — `.json`

A single `.json` file (any name) with the same four keys, values as nested lists:

```json
{
  "x_train": [[0.1, 0.5, ...], [0.3, 0.2, ...], ...],
  "y_train": [3, 7, 1, ...],
  "x_test":  [[0.4, 0.1, ...], ...],
  "y_test":  [5, 0, ...]
}
```

Slower to parse than `.pt` but human-readable and language-agnostic.

### Option 3 — `.csv` (two files)

Two CSV files in this directory:

- **`train.csv`** — training set plus its true labels.
- **`test.csv`** — held-out test set.

Each file must have:
- a header row (any column names — they're skipped on read);
- one example per row;
- the **last column** as the integer class label;
- all other columns as float features.

```
# train.csv
feat_0,feat_1,feat_2,...,feat_783,label
0.0,0.0,0.13,...,0.42,3
0.0,0.05,0.0,...,0.18,7
...
```

## Format requirements (all options)

- `x_train` and `x_test` must be 2D `(N, input_dim)`. Flatten image-shaped data
  before saving, OR swap in a CNN architecture in `method/model.py` and remove
  the flatten step in `method/data.py`.
- `y_train` and `y_test` must be 1D long tensors with values in `[0, n_classes)`.
- The student and teacher architectures in `method/model.py` infer `input_dim`
  from `x_train.shape[1]` and `n_classes` from `len(torch.unique(y_train))`,
  so any consistent choice works.

## Note on KD-specific data

Knowledge distillation typically uses the *same* training set for both the
student and teacher (the teacher is pretrained on this or a similar
distribution). If your teacher was trained on a different distribution, the
distillation signal may be weak — match the data domain where possible.
